<para>Then,</para>

<!--Q#6-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Identify the slope of the new line, \( n \). </para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>0</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->


<!--Q#7-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the equation of the new line, \( n \).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( y = -2 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<para>The original line \(l\) and the line \(m\) that you created are both parallel to the \(y\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(x\)-axis. It follows, then, that line n is parallel to the \(x\)-axis and perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis.</para>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q#8-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the equation of a line that is parallel to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((-2, -5)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
    <para>Enter your answer here:</para>
  <para>Compare your answer:</para>
    <para>\( x = -2 \)</para>
  </solution></exercise>
<!--Interaction End -->

<!--Q#9-->
<exercise>
  <problem>
    <para>Write the equation of a line that is perpendicular to the \(y\)-axis and passing through \((1, 5)\).</para>
    </problem><solution>
    
